1. In software engineering, “complex” means:

A. Hard to understand
B. Poorly documented
C. Composed of many simple parts related to one another
D. Impossible to solve
Answer: C

2. The “Frog in Boiling Water” analogy illustrates:

A. Rapid catastrophic failure
B. Gradual accumulation of small problems leading to major issues
C. Immediate system crashes
D. Agile development benefits
Answer: B

3. Accumulation of design smells and technical debt eventually leads to:

A. Refactoring
B. Software rot
C. Perfect architecture
D. Improved maintainability
Answer: B

4. According to the First Law of Software Engineering:

A. Code should be optimized first
B. Software engineers must learn the problem domain
C. Customers must understand programming
D. Design is optional
Answer: B

5. The software engineer acts primarily as:

A. A coder
B. A tester
C. A bridge between customer needs and programming implementation
D. A hardware technician
Answer: C

6. In the ATM example, the bank customer is considered:

A. A concept
B. A subsystem
C. An actor
D. A class
Answer: C

7. In system modeling, actors are:

A. Internal system components
B. External agents interacting with the system
C. Database tables
D. Hardware devices only
Answer: B

8. Concepts (or objects) in domain modeling are:

A. Always visible users
B. External systems
C. Internal agents that make the system function
D. Programming languages
Answer: C

9. Divide-and-conquer helps primarily by:

A. Increasing complexity
B. Eliminating requirements
C. Creating a model of the problem domain
D. Avoiding documentation
Answer: C

10. A use case represents:

A. A database schema
B. A scenario of how the system is used
C. A UML class diagram
D. A testing strategy
Answer: B

11. The Second Law of Software Engineering states that software should be written:

A. For computers first
B. For managers
C. For people first
D. For performance only
Answer: C

12. The Waterfall method is characterized by:

A. Continuous feedback loops
B. Random phase switching
C. Unidirectional phases with no way back
D. Daily releases
Answer: C

13. In Iterative + Incremental development:

A. The entire system is built at once
B. Functionality is developed in increments with feedback
C. No feedback is allowed
D. Testing happens only at the end
Answer: B

14. Agile differs from Waterfall because Agile:

A. Avoids documentation
B. Eliminates requirements
C. Relies heavily on continuous user feedback
D. Has no estimation
Answer: C

15. UML stands for:

A. Unified Modeling Logic
B. Universal Modeling Language
C. Unified Modeling Language
D. User Management Language
Answer: C

16. A UML class typically contains:

A. Only attributes
B. Only operations
C. Name, attributes, and operations
D. Requirements only
Answer: C

17. Informal hand-drawn diagrams are recommended:

A. At final project submission
B. During early design stages
C. For legal documentation
D. After deployment
Answer: B

18. Standardized UML diagrams should be created:

A. Before understanding the problem
B. When consensus on design is reached
C. Only by managers
D. At random times
Answer: B

19. In work estimation strategy, after making an initial guess, the next step is to:

A. Finalize the project duration
B. Do a little work to measure speed
C. Deploy immediately
D. Double the estimate
Answer: B

20. In sizing the problem, total size is calculated by:

A. Estimating one large task
B. Multiplying velocity by time
C. Summing relative sizes of all parts
D. Counting developers
Answer: C

21. Velocity refers to:

A. Code complexity
B. Speed of work completion
C. Number of developers
D. Testing coverage
Answer: B

22. According to the law of diminishing returns in estimation:

A. Accuracy improves infinitely at low cost
B. Improving estimation accuracy beyond a point requires huge effort
C. Estimation is always precise
D. Accuracy decreases over time

Answer: B

23. In the Waterfall method, estimation error:

A. Starts low and increases
B. Remains constant
C. Starts high and gradually converges toward zero
D. Is eliminated immediately
Answer: C

24. In Agile, estimation accuracy improves:

A. Only at the end
B. In leaps after completing iterations   (leap: bước nhảy lớn)
C. Without feedback
D. Randomly
Answer: B

25. Judging feasibility and quality of a design requires:

A. Only programming skill
B. No experience
C. Domain knowledge and commonsense
D. Automated tools only

Answer: C